  "slug": "untitled-topic-876d",
  "summary": "Recent preprints target specific error sources and resource bottlenecks in ptychography, single-pixel imaging, and hyperspectral microscopy.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread across these preprints is the recovery of optical information from imperfect or resource-limited measurements, addressed either computationally or through engineered acquisition. Two items concern ptychography. A stochastic gradient descent framework for X-ray ptychographic reconstruction reports peak GPU memory reduced to 65% and time reduced to 22% relative to the comparison method. Physics-simulation-trained residual learning restores diffraction patterns in MCP-based EUV ptychography, yielding an edge-response width of about 1.08 μm at 57 nm. In single-pixel imaging, a unified calibration and composite-readout framework converts temporal mixing into a measurement resource. Event-camera Fourier-transform hyperspectral microscopy with continuous birefringent interferometer scanning achieves real-time widefield spectral acquisition. The modalities differ; what is new in each is a targeted treatment of a specific error source or resource bottleneck.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
